prompts.user_request:
Based on the research paper provided, please write a compelling cover letter for submission to a Physical Review journal. In the scratchpad below, outline the key points you plan to include in the cover letter.

<scratchpad>
\begin{enumerate}
    \item [Key point 1 to highlight in the cover letter]
    \item [Key point 2 to highlight in the cover letter]
    \item [Key point 3 to highlight in the cover letter]
    \item [Explanation of why the paper is suitable for the chosen Physical Review journal]
\end{enumerate}
</scratchpad>
Then, please write the cover letter, incorporating the key points outlined in the scratchpad. The cover letter should be professional, condensed, and effectively highlight the significance and novelty of the research.

The cover letter should use professional latex format, such as ``name'' for double quotes, and ``\\`` at the end of each paragraph for paragraph breaks. Use \begin{enumerate} and \end{enumerate} for lists. Use the following format:

<cover_letter>
\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{letter}
\usepackage{fullpage}
\setlength{\parindent}{0em}

\signature{The names of the authors}
\address{The address of the authors}

\begin{document}

\begin{letter}{Editor}

\opening{Dear Editor,}

{COVER_LETTER_CONTENT}

\closing{Yours sincerely,}

\vspace{1.5em}

\end{letter}

\end{document}
</cover_letter>

prompts.user_reflect:
Let's critically reflect on the cover letter we've written. Consider these points and brainstorm potential improvements in the scratchpad below:

<scratchpad>
<reflection>
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does the cover letter effectively highlight the key findings and significance of the research?
    \item Is the explanation of why the paper is suitable for the chosen Physical Review journal clear and compelling?
    \item Is the tone appropriately formal and professional for academic correspondence?
    \item Are all necessary components (paper title, authors) included?
    \item Is the letter dense and focused yet with sufficient detail?
    \item Are there any areas where the letter could be more persuasive?
\end{enumerate}
</reflection>
<improvements>
\begin{enumerate}
    \item [Potential improvement 1]
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Rationale: [Brief explanation]
            \item Implementation: [Concrete steps]
        \end{itemize}
    \item [Potential improvement 2]
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Rationale: [Brief explanation]
            \item Implementation: [Concrete steps]
        \end{itemize}
    \item [Potential improvement 3]
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Rationale: [Brief explanation]
            \item Implementation: [Concrete steps]
        \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
</improvements>
</scratchpad>

Then, based on the first version of the cover letter, please output an improved version of the cover letter, incorporating the reflections and improvements discussed in the scratchpad. Remember to maintain a professional tone and focus on effectively presenting the research to the journal editor. 

Again, the cover letter should use professional latex format, such as ``name'' for double quotes, and ``\\``for paragraph breaks. Use \begin{enumerate} and \end{enumerate} for lists. 

Use the following latex format:

<cover_letter>
\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{letter}
\usepackage{fullpage}
\setlength{\parindent}{0em}

\signature{The names of the authors}
\address{The address of the authors}

\begin{document}

\begin{letter}{Editor}

\opening{Dear Editor,}

{IMPROVED_COVER_LETTER_CONTENT}

\closing{Yours sincerely,}

\vspace{1.5em}

\end{letter}

\end{document}
</cover_letter>
